    "text": "96\n\n How to make sure you get a good deal when buying or selling an accounting practice\n\n Watch a Lawyer Change Her Mind!\nHi Everyone,\nI\u2019m Sara Sharp, and I help people buy and sell accounting firms. After years of helping people with these deals, I've actually learned a new angle on one of the oldest terms out there: exclusivity. \nMy Old Way of Thinking: Exclusivity isn't Enforceable So Who Cares\nHistorically I've noticed people don't even try to enforce an exclusivity provision because the dollar amount of damages is too low to justify hiring lawyer to fight it out (at least in these sub $5M deals).\nThe New Thought: Exclusivity is Can Be Pretty Powerful\nYou've heard me talk about the narrow sales window in these deals-spoiler it's SHORT. Basically a couple of months in the summer between tax seasons. By being exclusive for a short period of time, you can motivate people to push through diligence as fast as possible. \nWhat if You Combine it With a Good Faith Deposit?\nImagine you're a seller and you stop hearing from your buyer very often. Maybe they've lost the enthusiasm for the deal or they have a few under LOI and they're waiting to see how to allocate capital before they decide which deals to actually go through. What motivation do they have to cut you loose? \nUsing Deposit + Exclusivity to = Efficiency\nAs you know, I hammer the drum of efficiency in small business deals. You're running a business, you don't have time to waste. Plus the only person who wins in an efficient deal process is the lawyer (tongue firmly in cheek). \nThe Point of the Good Faith Deposit\nLet's say the buyer submits a $15,000 good faith deposit tied to the seller agreeing to be exclusive for 60 days. \nSo...\nAs the deal is waning in day 30, you suddenly have a motivation to let the other side know you're calling it so you can get your money back. Even more nuanced, the buyer has the sudden and significant motivation to admit it to themselves. This serves efficiency and frees both parties up to pursue a deal that'll actually close. GASP. So let me know, did I change your mind about the usefulness of these terms? Even just a little bit?\nThank you for being part of this journey. Have a wonderful weekend, and don\u2019t forget to enjoy the little things.\nBest,\nSara Sharp \nDeal Attorney\nPartner, SK&S Law Group\nP.S. If you have any questions about your next acquisition or sale, feel free to reach out for a consult call. I\u2019m here to help you make your next deal a great one.\n--\nFor more helpful lessons from Deal academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out our latest Dealmakers podcast episodes by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2024\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
